WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.736 --> 00:00:21.147
The next morning I arose at dawn and occupied myself for an hour or two arranging my things

2
00:00:21.207 --> 00:00:23.149
in preparation for a brief absence.

3
00:00:24.369 --> 00:00:25.050
I heard St.

4
00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.532
John leave his room and stop outside my door.

5
00:00:28.672 --> 00:00:32.295
I feared that he would knock, but he merely slipped a note under my door.

6
00:00:34.156 --> 00:00:36.037
Jane—You left me too suddenly last night.

7
00:00:37.278 --> 00:00:41.941
If you had stayed only a little longer, you would have chosen the life of a missionary's wife.

8
00:00:43.122 --> 00:00:45.803
I'll expect your final decision when I return in two weeks.

9
00:00:47.084 --> 00:00:50.847
Meanwhile I will pray for you hourly.—Yours, St.

10
00:00:50.867 --> 00:00:56.550
John Although it was June, the morning was overcast and chilly, with a driving rain.

11
00:00:57.731 --> 00:00:59.192
I heard the front door close.

12
00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:02.494
Looking out my window, I saw St.

13
00:01:02.514 --> 00:01:06.098
John headed in the direction of Whitcross, where he would meet the coach.

14
00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.242
I, too, would be meeting a coach at Whitcross that day.

15
00:01:10.262 --> 00:01:17.190
"Before long I will know something of him whose voice seemed to summon me last night," I said

16
00:01:17.230 --> 00:01:17.790
to myself.

17
00:01:19.052 --> 00:01:22.996
"My letters have been fruitless, so I will make inquiries in person."

18
00:01:24.240 --> 00:01:28.703
At breakfast I announced to Diana and Mary that I was going on a journey and would be

19
00:01:28.883 --> 00:01:30.524
absent at least four days.

20
00:01:31.724 --> 00:01:37.288
They asked few questions, only wondering if I were well enough to undertake a trip because I looked

21
00:01:37.348 --> 00:01:37.968
so pale.

22
00:01:39.229 --> 00:01:44.952
I replied that nothing bothered me except anxiety about a friend and I hoped to alleviate that soon.

23
00:01:46.213 --> 00:01:49.855
At four o'clock that afternoon, I stood at the signpost in Whitcross.

24
00:01:51.055 --> 00:01:55.599
The coach arrived—the same vehicle that had left me here nearly a year ago.

25
00:01:56.840 --> 00:02:03.365
After a journey of thirty-six hours, I arrived at the Rochester Arms, an inn only two miles from

26
00:02:03.405 --> 00:02:07.368
Thornfield. I could have asked someone at the inn about Mr.

27
00:02:07.428 --> 00:02:11.852
Rochester, but I dreaded the reply, so I set off for Thornfield on foot.

28
00:02:13.814 --> 00:02:14.634
Perhaps Mr.

29
00:02:14.694 --> 00:02:17.937
Rochester would be standing at his window or walking in the orchard.

30
00:02:19.118 --> 00:02:21.340
Or perhaps he wasn't even in England anymore.

31
00:02:22.528 --> 00:02:27.691
Passing along the lower wall of the orchard, I cautiously peeked around the great stone pillar of a

32
00:02:27.711 --> 00:02:34.015
gate. Expecting to view the front of the mansion, I was stunned to see a blackened ruin.

33
00:02:35.155 --> 00:02:37.397
No wonder that my letters had received no answer.

34
00:02:38.557 --> 00:02:42.700
A fire had obviously destroyed Thornfield, but how had it started?

35
00:02:44.261 --> 00:02:45.461
I hurried back to the inn.

36
00:02:46.602 --> 00:02:49.524
When the innkeeper brought my breakfast, I asked him to sit with me.

37
00:02:50.636 --> 00:02:55.079
But I hardly knew where to begin, so worried was I about his potential answers.

38
00:02:56.280 --> 00:02:57.721
"You know Thornfield, of course?"

39
00:02:58.121 --> 00:02:59.582
I finally managed to say.

40
00:02:59.602 --> 00:03:02.624
"Yes, I was the late Mr.

41
00:03:02.705 --> 00:03:03.725
Rochester's butler."

42
00:03:03.745 --> 00:03:06.127
"The late!"

43
00:03:06.627 --> 00:03:07.208
I gasped.

44
00:03:07.228 --> 00:03:08.949
"Is he dead?"

45
00:03:08.969 --> 00:03:10.890
"I mean Mr.

46
00:03:10.990 --> 00:03:14.953
Edward Rochester's father," he explained, and I breathed again.

47
00:03:14.973 --> 00:03:16.794
"Is Mr.

48
00:03:16.854 --> 00:03:18.816
Rochester living at Thornfield now?"

49
00:03:20.004 --> 00:03:22.566
I knew the answer to that question but asked it anyway.

50
00:03:22.586 --> 00:03:24.227
"Oh no.

51
00:03:25.328 --> 00:03:29.250
You must be a stranger or you'd know that Thornfield burned down last autumn.

52
00:03:30.371 --> 00:03:32.252
The fire broke out in the dead of night."

53
00:03:32.272 --> 00:03:34.174
"The dead of night!"

54
00:03:35.394 --> 00:03:38.096
That was always the hour for trouble at Thornfield.

55
00:03:38.116 --> 00:03:40.658
"Does anyone know how it started?"

56
00:03:41.919 --> 00:03:46.082
The innkeeper edged his chair a little nearer to the table and spoke softly.

57
00:03:46.102 --> 00:03:49.304
"There was an insane woman in the house.

58
00:03:50.608 --> 00:03:55.831
No one ever saw her, but there had always been rumors, and it turned out she was Mr.

59
00:03:55.911 --> 00:03:56.852
Rochester's wife!

60
00:03:58.873 --> 00:04:04.536
That news was discovered when a young governess—" I dreaded hearing my own story.

61
00:04:04.556 --> 00:04:06.317
"But the fire?"

62
00:04:06.337 --> 00:04:08.378
"I'm coming to that.

63
00:04:09.579 --> 00:04:12.181
Mr. Edward fell in love with this young governess.

64
00:04:12.781 --> 00:04:16.003
The servants said they never saw anybody so in love as he was.

65
00:04:17.144 --> 00:04:20.665
He used to—" "You can tell me that story later.

66
00:04:21.826 --> 00:04:25.428
Was there any suspicion that this insane woman was responsible for the fire?"

67
00:04:25.448 --> 00:04:28.429
"It was quite certain that she set it."

68
00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:31.291
He went on to tell me the story.

69
00:04:32.471 --> 00:04:36.654
Mrs. Poole was in charge of the insane wife, but sometimes when Mrs.

70
00:04:36.734 --> 00:04:38.675
Poole was fast asleep, Mrs.

71
00:04:38.775 --> 00:04:40.155
Rochester roamed the house.

72
00:04:41.456 --> 00:04:47.019
That night she set fire to the bed in the next room and then went downstairs and burned

73
00:04:47.059 --> 00:04:48.480
the bed in the governess' room.

74
00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:53.381
"Fortunately the governess had run away a few months before.

75
00:04:54.601 --> 00:04:59.042
Mr. Rochester had sought her as if she were the most precious thing in the world, but he

76
00:04:59.102 --> 00:05:00.463
never heard a word about her.

77
00:05:01.663 --> 00:05:05.284
He wanted to be alone after that, so he sent his housekeeper, Mrs.

78
00:05:05.364 --> 00:05:09.945
Fairfax, to live with relatives, and he sent his ward, Miss Adele, to school."

79
00:05:11.186 --> 00:05:13.086
"So he was at home when the fire broke out?"

80
00:05:13.106 --> 00:05:15.127
"Yes indeed.

81
00:05:16.247 --> 00:05:19.508
He went upstairs to get his wife and make sure that the servants were safe.

82
00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:23.041
Then they called out to him that his wife was on the roof.

83
00:05:24.321 --> 00:05:28.362
Mr. Rochester went up there to rescue her, but she jumped to her death."

84
00:05:29.643 --> 00:05:30.663
"How horrible!

85
00:05:31.783 --> 00:05:33.204
Were any other lives lost?"

86
00:05:33.224 --> 00:05:36.765
"No, but it might have been better if there had been."

87
00:05:36.785 --> 00:05:39.346
"What do you mean?"

88
00:05:39.766 --> 00:05:40.246
I asked.

89
00:05:40.266 --> 00:05:42.867
"Mr. Edward is blind."

90
00:05:44.127 --> 00:05:50.389
I had feared worse—that he was insane—but it still took all my courage to ask what had caused

91
00:05:50.429 --> 00:05:51.230
this calamity.

92
00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:54.811
"He wouldn't leave the house until everyone was out.

93
00:05:56.072 --> 00:05:59.313
As he came down the great staircase, the house crashed around him.

94
00:06:00.533 --> 00:06:05.175
He lost an eye and a hand, and his other eye was so inflamed that he lost the

95
00:06:05.275 --> 00:06:06.376
sight in that one too."

96
00:06:07.516 --> 00:06:08.216
"Where is he?"

97
00:06:08.637 --> 00:06:09.097
I cried.

98
00:06:09.117 --> 00:06:14.839
"At Ferndean, a manor house on a farm that he owns about thirty miles from here."

99
00:06:14.859 --> 00:06:16.820
"Do you have a carriage?

100
00:06:18.256 --> 00:06:21.759
I will pay twice your usual rate if you can get me to Ferndean by nightfall."

101
00:06:22.900 --> 00:06:27.104
The innkeeper left to make the arrangements, and I was soon on my way to Ferndean.

102
00:06:29.065 --> 00:06:33.769
I walked the last mile on foot and reached the house as daylight faded and a light rain

103
00:06:33.849 --> 00:06:39.995
fell. So dank and green were its decaying walls that it was difficult to distinguish the house from

104
00:06:40.035 --> 00:06:41.316
the trees that surrounded it.

105
00:06:42.557 --> 00:06:46.260
Just then the door opened, and a figure stepped outside.

106
00:06:47.508 --> 00:06:51.669
Cautiously he went down one step and walked forward a few paces.

107
00:06:52.930 --> 00:06:55.290
He stretched out his hand as if to feel the rain.

108
00:06:56.571 --> 00:07:00.112
It was my master, Edward Fairfax Rochester!

109
00:07:01.392 --> 00:07:04.653
I watched him as he stood in the rain, now falling fast.

110
00:07:05.933 --> 00:07:07.714
His servant, John, appeared at the door.

111
00:07:07.734 --> 00:07:09.995
"You should come in, sir.

112
00:07:11.175 --> 00:07:12.235
Will you take my arm?"

113
00:07:13.516 --> 00:07:14.196
"Leave me alone."

114
00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:18.802
Mr. Rochester groped his way back into the house and closed the door.

115
00:07:20.103 --> 00:07:23.285
I took a deep breath as I approached the door and knocked.
